 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected for its repeated reads and seconds of lag despite simpler stateless operation. A hosted realtime service was rejected for recurring fees and provider coupling despite needing fewer connection operations. Self-hosted sockets deliver fast updates without a realtime vendor; the accepted cost is owning connection capacity and reconnect operations. See [change-delivery-approach](decisions/change-delivery-approach.md) for the full record.
